### Database connections

Every process holds a pool of connections, and PostgreSQL has a hard limit. The plan needs about
**{{ sizing.total_db_connections }}** connections against a budget of **{{ sizing.connection_budget }}**
({{ (sizing.connection_budget * 100 // p.scale.db_max_connections) }} percent of `max_connections={{ p.scale.db_max_connections }}`).
{% if p.scale.pgbouncer %}
PgBouncer is on: the application opens many connections to PgBouncer, which keeps only
**{{ sizing.pgbouncer_pool_size }}** open to PostgreSQL. It runs in transaction mode, so the
application sets `DB_PGBOUNCER=true` (prepared statements off). Do not use session-level features
(`SET`, advisory locks held across transactions, `LISTEN`) through it; this code does not.
{% else %}
There is no PgBouncer. If you add replicas beyond this plan, add up
`replicas × server workers × (pool + overflow)` for the API and `replicas × pool` for the workers,
plus about 6 for maintenance, and keep the total under {{ sizing.connection_budget }}.
{% endif %}

